	volume = {68},
	issn = {0392-4866},
	url = {https://www.jstor.org/stable/41880816},
	abstract = {While working on the translation of the Thesaurus Inscriptionum Eblaicarum, many philological and lexical questions have been raised: the present contribution, which will be followed by orther articles on the topic of the lexicology of Ebla, offers an etymology for two words which are to be found in the Ebla texts. The first one is the Eblaic translation ʾà-da-ru₁₂-um of the lexeme «šà» which one encounters in the VE. According to the proposed interpretation the word doesn't mean «heart», but is the name of a textile. The second word, ʾà-lum, which is documented in the economic tablets, is related to the word «a-lum» which accompanies «udu-mar-tu» in the Sumerian texts (chiefly from the period of the Ur III Dinasty).},
